99:/// D-2026-08-20-3 (owner: warn, don't refuse): an input that falls
100:/// through to LOCAL but whose first path segment is shaped like a
101:/// daemon address (`host:port`) is almost always a typo'd remote —
102:/// `host:port//path` where the root-export form is `host:port://path`.
103:/// The loopback smoke that earned this rule watched
104:/// `127.0.0.1:9911//dst1/` silently materialize a literal
105:/// `./127.0.0.1:9911/dst1` directory and report success. Classification
106:/// is deliberately unchanged (no local-wins ambiguity, same stance as
107:/// the 2026-07-12 bare-name ruling); this only names the trap out loud.
108:/// Returns the warning text so tests can pin the shape detection.
109:fn remote_shaped_local_warning(input: &str) -> Option<String> {
110:    let first = input.split(['/', '\\']).next().unwrap_or("");
111:    let (host, port) = first.rsplit_once(':')?;
112:    if host.is_empty() || port.is_empty() || !port.bytes().all(|b| b.is_ascii_digit()) {
113:        return None;
114:    }
115:    Some(format!(
116:        "'{input}' is being treated as a LOCAL path, but '{first}' looks like a \
117:         daemon address — for a remote transfer use '{host}:{port}://<path>' \
118:         (root export) or '{host}:{port}:/<module>/<path>'; use './{input}' to \
119:         silence this warning"
120:    ))
121:}
