833:/// Take one shard member's verdict *where its failure happened* — inside
834:/// the worker that wrote it, while the destination is still in the state
835:/// that write saw (cr-pfc3-2).
836:///
837:/// This is the only place a shard member is classified, and it decides
838:/// through the same [`failure_is_containable`] predicate the single-file
839:/// paths use, so the shard writers can never drift from them on what is
840:/// per-file and what is session-fatal.
841:pub(super) fn classify_shard_member<S>(
842:    dst_root: &Path,
843:    relative_path: &str,
844:    result: std::result::Result<(u64, S), FailedWrite>,
845:) -> ClassifiedMember<S> {
846:    match result {
847:        Ok((bytes, sample)) => ClassifiedMember::Written(bytes, sample),
848:        Err(failed) if failure_is_containable(dst_root, relative_path, &failed.error) => {
849:            ClassifiedMember::Contained {
850:                error: failed.error,
851:                removed: failed.removed,
852:            }
853:        }
854:        Err(failed) => ClassifiedMember::Fatal(failed.error),
855:    }
856:}
