686:/// Raw OS error codes that mean "this volume refuses writes" rather
687:/// than "this one path was refused" — write-protection and exhaustion
688:/// alike. Checked alongside the portable [`std::io::ErrorKind`]
689:/// spellings ([`std::io::ErrorKind::ReadOnlyFilesystem`],
690:/// [`std::io::ErrorKind::StorageFull`],
691:/// [`std::io::ErrorKind::QuotaExceeded`]): the kinds are what std names,
692:/// the numeric codes are what the OS actually returned and stay correct
693:/// even where a platform's std mapping does not name the kind. The code
694:/// namespaces are per-platform and overlap with unrelated meanings —
695:/// unix 19 is `ENODEV` where Windows 19 is `ERROR_WRITE_PROTECT`, unix
696:/// 30 is `EROFS` where Windows 30 is `ERROR_READ_FAULT`, unix 39 is
697:/// `ENOTEMPTY` (an ordinary per-file error) where Windows 39 is
698:/// `ERROR_HANDLE_DISK_FULL` — so each list is cfg-gated to its own
699:/// platform instead of merged into one set.
700:///
701:/// `EDQUOT` is deliberately absent from the raw lists: its number is not
702:/// portable across unix (122 on Linux, 69 on macOS/BSD), so one
703:/// unix-wide list would either miss it or claim an unrelated code, while
704:/// std maps it to [`std::io::ErrorKind::QuotaExceeded`] on every unix.
705:#[cfg(unix)]
706:const VOLUME_UNWRITABLE_OS_ERRORS: &[i32] = &[
707:    30, // EROFS
708:    28, // ENOSPC
709:];
710:#[cfg(windows)]
711:const VOLUME_UNWRITABLE_OS_ERRORS: &[i32] = &[
712:    19,  // ERROR_WRITE_PROTECT
713:    112, // ERROR_DISK_FULL
714:    39,  // ERROR_HANDLE_DISK_FULL
715:];
716:#[cfg(not(any(unix, windows)))]
717:const VOLUME_UNWRITABLE_OS_ERRORS: &[i32] = &[];
