734:/// cr-pfc2-1 + cr-pfc3-1: true when `error`'s chain carries a
735:/// volume-level unwritability signal — read-only filesystem,
736:/// write-protected medium, exhausted volume or quota.
737:/// That is a root-wide condition wearing a per-file error's clothes:
738:/// [`destination_root_live`] sees a perfectly good directory (a
739:/// read-only mount still reads as one), so without this check every
740:/// payload's write failure would be contained and a mirror to a
741:/// read-only mount would write nothing, delete nothing — a write-failed
742:/// file stays in the source manifest, so it is never extraneous — and
743:/// report success. The whole chain is walked because every write site
744:/// wraps its `io::Error` in `with_context` before the classifier sees
745:/// it.
746:///
747:/// Exhaustion (cr-pfc3-1) belongs to the same class: a destination that
748:/// fills mid-run fails every remaining payload identically — inside a
749:/// tar shard that is potentially thousands of members — so containing
750:/// those would let a mirror report success over a demonstrably
751:/// incomplete backup. The deliberate trade is that a transient `ENOSPC`
752:/// (space freed again later in the same run) loses per-file
753:/// continuation; re-running converges once there is room, whereas a
754:/// falsely successful mirror does not.
755:fn volume_unwritable(error: &eyre::Report) -> bool {
756:    error.chain().any(|cause| {
757:        cause
758:            .downcast_ref::<std::io::Error>()
759:            .is_some_and(io_error_says_volume_unwritable)
760:    })
761:}
